+\section{SDK follow-up on numbered stores}\label{app:sdkfollowup}
+
+The main experiments retain the evaluated read path, prompts and scores reported
+above. This follow-up compares saved public-0.1.0 reads with the native 0.1.1 SDK
+on FactConsolidation, at the same ten-unit retrieval budget. We designed the two
+rules on FactConsolidation and measure them on its numbered fact stores.
+
+\paragraph{Subject--relation slots and reading order.}
+In the native SDK, dot-numbered records can use a slot consisting of the subject
+and relation with the value removed. A relation qualifies when at least two
+subjects share it. If any subjects have more than one distinct line for that
+relation, their mean line count must be at most four. Other records keep the
+0.1.0 slot rule (Appendix~\ref{app:slotsrc}). From the subjects a question names,
+the walk follows the subjects each reached record names and shows the reached
+records, hop by hop, before the rest of the selection in serial order.
+The walk reranks at most 400 lines
+across up to four rounds, alongside the reranking in Algorithm~\ref{alg:muster}.
+
+\paragraph{Protocol and results.}
+We use the official FactConsolidation answer prompt and score the first ten
+\texttt{o200k\_base} tokens of the visible answer, after removing
+\texttt{<think>} blocks, with the benchmark's substring match.
+There are 100 single-hop and 100 multi-hop questions at each of 6K, 32K and 64K
+tokens, and a separate 100 of each at 262K. Table~\ref{tab:sdk011fc} reports all
+questions, including unanswered ones as incorrect. Mean first-pass prompt usage
+ranges from 339 to 357 tokens for 0.1.0 and from 340 to 360 for 0.1.1
+across the six reader-by-scale cells.
+
